<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Pesaro</h1><p class="st-meta">1285 CE – 1512 CE (approx.) · capital Pesaro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Adriatic lordship held by the Malatesta and then the Sforza, before passing to the della Rovere dukes of Urbino in 1512.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-family">the Malatesta</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-rimini">Rimini</a> took control of Pesaro in 1285 and ruled it as lords and papal vicars. In 1445 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-malatesta">Galeazzo Malatesta</a> sold Pesaro to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-sforza">Alessandro Sforza</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, who had married his granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costanza-da-varano">Costanza da Varano</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-family">the Malatesta</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">Sforza</a>, Pesaro was a small Adriatic court state living from farming, fishing and maiolica pottery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-sforza">Alessandro Sforza</a> patronised artists, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costanzo-i-sforza">Costanzo</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rocca-costanza">Rocca Costanza</a> fortress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-sforza">Giovanni Sforza</a>, Costanzo's son, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucrezia-borgia">Lucrezia Borgia</a>, but the marriage was annulled and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> expelled him in 1500. He returned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI's</a> death in 1503.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Giovanni's son died young, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> granted Pesaro in 1512 to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-maria-i-della-rovere">Francesco Maria I della Rovere</a>, joining it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-urbino">Duchy of Urbino</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1285 CE – 1512 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.27%;width:4.52%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1285 CE – 1512 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pesaro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alessandro Sforza, Costanzo Sforza, Giovanni Sforza</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pesaro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=lordship-of-pesaro" aria-label="Lordship of Pesaro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4990 622 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Pesaro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5310 878L5304 942M5310 878L5304 884"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5304 884h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5310 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5310 878h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pesaro. <a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=lordship-of-pesaro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
